red@redpoll.mrfs.oh.us (Richard E. Depew) writes:
>|    DEFAMATION  An act of communication that causes someone to be shamed,
>|    ridiculed, held in contempt, or lowered in the estimation of the
>|    community, or to lose employment status or earnings or otherwise
>|    suffer a damaged reputation.  Such defamation is couched in
>|    _defamatory language_...

>|    LIBEL  Published material ... meeting three conditions: (1) the
>|    material is *defamatory* either on its face or indirectly; (2) the
>|    defamatory statement is about someone who is *identifiable* to one or
>|    more persons; and (3) the material must be distributed to someone
>|    other than the offended party; i.e., published.

Obviously there is something missing from your definition. Let me give you an 
example: Suppose a person, lets call him Bob, comes along tomorrow and based on
personal views starts canceling other peoples posts. The say that Bob starts
bombing newsgroups with autoposters. After a while Bob mellows down a bit. Now
a year goes by. Bob decides he wants to be the moderator of a new group:
net.standards.and.practices and is trying to get support for himself. I think
it would be a terrible idea and post a message saying, "Hey, we don't want this
guy for a moderator, he's the guy who pulled all that stuff last year." My
post will certainly cause Bob to be lowered in the estimation of the community.
I have specifily identified Bob to the net at large, and I have posted to the
net at large. So by your definition, I have libeled Bob. In fact, any post that
criticizes another person is libel by your definition. In fact, all or Cosar
posts calling Davidson a libeler are libel since they fit your definition. I
guess you feel that it would be ok by you for Davidson to start canceling them.
Maybe you need to add something to this definition.

>     Yes, this presumption does accept the possibility of unwarrented
>cancels in the case when the victim is, in fact, guilty as accused.
>Because attacks on one's character are so dangerous, the burden of
>proof must remain with the accuser.
>
Two problems with this:

1) Proof is in the eye of the beholder. What the "libeler" might consider
proof, the "libelee" might just consider to be more libel. The problem is that
since you have thrown out objectivity, you can't count on people agreeing with
each other, about what "proof", or "libel" is.

2) Nothing in your definition says anything to the guilt or innocese of the
accused person. A guilty person can still have his reputation ruined. Sure,
the legal definition of Libel say something about guilt, but yours doesnt.

Michael




